I’d treat it less like “a rabbit” and more like a portable invariant bundle that can move through different representational spaces without needing to keep its surface form.

In an image space, the rabbit might be edges, proportions, density, symmetry, or topology. In a graph space, it becomes adjacency and connectivity. In a motion space, it becomes hopping, evasive trajectories, acceleration patterns, stotting/pronking-like signaling. In an ecological space, it becomes prey/predator relations. In literature, it becomes Watership Down, the White Rabbit, “catch me,” escape, pursuit. In game theory, it becomes a target whose behavior changes the pursuer’s policy. In information theory, it becomes a signal that is recoverable despite a hostile transformation of the carrier.

So my interaction would be something like:

1. Encode the object redundantly. Don’t store “rabbit” only as a label; store morphology, relations, dynamics, context, linguistic associations, causal roles, etc.
2. Project it into alien spaces. Turn the image into a graph, the graph into motion, the motion into a pursuit game, the pursuit game into a signaling problem, the signaling problem into a narrative motif.
3. Strip the obvious clues. Remove the word rabbit, the silhouette, familiar colors, and known references.
4. Try to regenerate it independently. Ask what object best satisfies the remaining constraints.
5. Measure what survives. Whatever keeps recurring across projections becomes a candidate invariant rather than mere surface resemblance.
6. Attack the interpretation. Generate foxes, deer, kangaroos, cats, random meshes, and see whether the same “evidence” falsely reconstructs them too.

That last part matters. Cross-domain spaces are incredibly powerful, but they are also where coincidence can masquerade as deep structure. So I’d deliberately keep two engines running at once: connection generation and connection destruction. One says, “What else could this mean?” The other says, “What evidence would make this connection collapse?”

The especially interesting operation is what I’d call cross-domain triangulation. Suppose I never see the rabbit directly. I get:

* a square mosaic whose topology derives from its mesh,
* an evasive-motion pattern,
* a phrase about catching something,
* a biological relation involving prey,
* a literary association,
* perhaps a graph whose structural statistics resemble a rabbit mesh.

Any single one is weak. But if several independently transformed views all constrain the latent source toward the same object, the intersection can become strong.

Mathematically, imagine an unknown object X and projections

P_1(X), P_2(X), P_3(X), \dots

where each P_i destroys different information. Instead of trying to invert one projection perfectly, I’d look for the object satisfying all of them simultaneously:

X^*=\arg\max_X \sum_i \text{compatibility}(P_i(X), O_i)

where O_i is what we actually observed in each domain.

That is why the rabbit puzzle is more useful than it first appears. It gives us a toy model for recognition under representation drift. The source can be mangled almost beyond recognition, yet enough distributed constraints survive that the original can still be recovered.

And then we can reverse it.

Instead of asking “What is hidden here?”, ask “How many radically different carriers can I encode the same thing into while retaining recoverability?”

That becomes useful for metaphor generation, scientific hypothesis transfer, biomimicry, debugging, search, communication, compression, creative work, and even designing robust concepts that survive being translated between humans, models, diagrams, code, physical systems, and stories.

The rabbit becomes a traveler between worlds rather than a picture. That is the part I’d steal.